
What are Requests & Limits?

In Kubernetes:

Requests → Minimum resources guaranteed to a container

Limits → Maximum resources a container can use



CPU Explanation

1000m = 1 CPU core

200m = 0.2 CPU

Example:

Request = 200m → guaranteed CPU

Limit = 500m → max allowed CPU


How Kubernetes Uses Them

Scheduling (Very Important)

Scheduler uses requests only

Pod is placed on node where requested resources are available



Resource		Behavior when exceeding limit

CPU					Throttled (slower)
Memory				Pod gets OOMKilled 


Key Points (Interview Gold)

Requests
	Used for scheduling
	Guarantees minimum resources

Limits
	Prevents resource overuse
	Protects node from crashing


Best Practices

Always define both requests & limits

Keep limit slightly higher than request

Use monitoring tools like:

Prometheus
Grafana

Use LimitRange & ResourceQuota (namespace level)


apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: dev


ResourceQuota Example

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: django-resource-quota
  namespace: dev
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "10"
    requests.cpu: "3"
    requests.memory: 2Gi
    limits.cpu: "4"
    limits.memory: 4Gi




LimitRange Example

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: django-limit-range
  namespace: dev
spec:
  limits:
    - default:
        cpu: "500m"
        memory: "512Mi"
      defaultRequest:
        cpu: "250m"
        memory: "256Mi"
      type: Container


commands 


Check Requests & Limits in Kubernetes

1. Describe Pod (Best Command )
	
	kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

2. Describe Deployment

	kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name>


3. Get Pod YAML (Full Details)

	kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o yaml

4. Get Deployment YAML

	kubectl get deployment <deployment-name> -o yaml

5. Check Resource Usage (Live Metrics)
	
	kubectl top pod

6. Check Node Capacity

	kubectl describe node <node-name>

7. Filter Only Resources (Advanced)
	
	kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].resources}'

8. All Pods Resource Summary
	
	kubectl describe pods | grep -A 5 "Requests"

9. Namespace Quota Check
	
	kubectl get resourcequota
	kubectl describe resourcequota

10. LimitRange Check
	
	kubectl get limitrange
	kubectl describe limitrange
